# Business Question Queries

Queries used to answer the two business questions from the case study.

## 1. Active Customers

### Active Orders Count (Validation)

```sql
WITH order_types AS (
    SELECT order_id, LIST(DISTINCT event_type) AS types
    FROM read_json_auto('data/processed/events.json')
    GROUP BY order_id
)
SELECT COUNT(*) AS active_orders
FROM order_types
WHERE list_contains(types, 'order_paid')
  AND NOT list_contains(types, 'order_delivered')
  AND NOT list_contains(types, 'order_cancelled')
  AND NOT list_contains(types, 'order_refunded');
```

Finding:
133 active orders...

### Active Customers Count

An order is considered active if it has been paid, but has not yet been delivered, cancelled, or refunded.

```sql
SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT customer_id) AS active_customers
FROM read_json_auto('data/processed/order_summary.json')
WHERE is_active = true;
```

Finding:
113 customers currently have an active order (lower than the 133 active orders, since a single customer can have more than one active order at a time).

## 2. Orders Shipped in 2024 by Category

### Orders Shipped in 2024, by Category

Filters cleaned events down to `order_shipped` events that occurred in 2024, then unnests the `items` array. Orders are counted as distinct per category.

```sql
WITH shipped AS (
    SELECT order_id, items
    FROM read_json_auto('data/processed/events.json')
    WHERE event_type = 'order_shipped'
      AND EXTRACT(YEAR FROM CAST(event_timestamp AS TIMESTAMP)) = 2024
),
unique_orders AS (
    SELECT DISTINCT order_id, item.category AS category
    FROM shipped,
         UNNEST(items) AS t(item)
)
SELECT category, COUNT(*) AS orders_shipped
FROM unique_orders
GROUP BY category
ORDER BY orders_shipped DESC;
```

